---
import TopicPage from "~/layouts/TopicPage.astro";
import { VERTICALS } from "~/data/verticals";

const v = VERTICALS.business;
---

<TopicPage
  vertical={v}
  topic="By state"
  headline="Business insurance by state"
  lede="Commercial insurance is less state-variable than personal lines, but workers' compensation rules, professional licensure requirements, and state-specific coverage mandates can matter for your business."
  sections={[
    {
      heading: "What varies by state",
      body: `
        <p><strong>Workers' compensation</strong> is the most state-variable commercial coverage. Every state has its own workers' comp system:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>State-funded systems (North Dakota, Ohio, Washington, Wyoming) — you can't buy private workers' comp; you buy from the state fund</li>
          <li>Competitive states — private carriers compete; you can choose</li>
          <li>Monopolistic vs. competitive states differ on price, claim handling, and rate-setting</li>
          <li>Independent contractor classification rules vary significantly state to state</li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>Professional licensure</strong> affects E&O insurance requirements. Many regulated professions (real estate, financial advising, legal, medical) have state-specific minimum insurance requirements.</p>
        <p><strong>Commercial auto</strong> follows personal-auto state regulations — minimum liability requirements vary, and some states require additional coverages for commercial fleets.</p>
        <p><strong>Cyber liability and data breach notification laws</strong> are state-specific. All 50 states have breach notification laws, but the triggers, timelines, and required actions differ.</p>
      `,
    },
    {
      heading: "What doesn't vary much",
      body: `
        <p>General liability, BOP, commercial property, and most professional liability policies use similar policy forms across states. National carriers price these largely the same coast to coast (adjusted for catastrophe risk on property).</p>
        <p>If you're a service business operating in one state, the state matters more for workers' comp than for general liability.</p>
      `,
    },
    {
      heading: "States with notable commercial considerations",
      body: `
        <h3>California</h3>
        <p>Strong wage-and-hour laws make EPLI more critical. AB5 / Prop 22 affects independent contractor classification. Higher workers' comp premiums driven by claim frequency and litigation. Cumulative trauma claims are unique to California in their breadth.</p>
        <h3>New York</h3>
        <p>Disability benefits law (DBL) and Paid Family Leave (PFL) are separate state-mandated coverages. Workers' comp rates are high. Strict insurance regulator (NY DFS).</p>
        <h3>Florida</h3>
        <p>No state income tax incentivizes operations. Workers' comp generally manageable. Hurricane exposure on commercial property is the main concern for businesses with physical locations.</p>
        <h3>Texas</h3>
        <p>The only state where workers' compensation is technically optional for private employers — though most still carry it for liability protection. Strong contractor/construction market with specialty carriers.</p>
        <h3>Washington / Ohio / North Dakota / Wyoming</h3>
        <p>State-monopoly workers' comp — you buy from the state fund, not private carriers. Private workers' comp brokers can still help with stop-gap policies and other commercial coverages.</p>
      `,
    },
    {
      heading: "How to navigate state differences",
      body: `
        <p>If you operate in multiple states or have employees in multiple states, work with a commercial broker who has multi-state experience. State workers' comp coverage is required separately in each state where you have employees, and misalignment between your policy state and your employees' state can leave you exposed.</p>
        <p>For most single-state small businesses, state considerations come down to:</p>
        <ol>
          <li>Workers' comp (mandatory, state-rated)</li>
          <li>Whether your industry has state licensure or insurance mandates</li>
          <li>Whether your physical location has catastrophe exposure (hurricane, earthquake, wildfire)</li>
        </ol>
      `,
    },
  ]}
  related={[
    { label: "Best business insurance", href: "/business-insurance/best/", desc: "Top-ranked carriers by industry" },
    { label: "Cheapest business insurance", href: "/business-insurance/cheapest/", desc: "Who competes on price by industry" },
    { label: "How much do I need?", href: "/business-insurance/how-much-do-i-need/", desc: "Right-sizing each coverage" },
    { label: "Coverage types", href: "/business-insurance/coverage-types/", desc: "Each commercial coverage explained" },
  ]}
/>
